{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FQPWoxVHk3vR6UihrG1GWA","type":"tool_result","content":"## B06 Approval integrity G1 | NA: None\nTie each approval to the agent instance that asked, and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope. Record who approved it and when it expires. Show the reviewer what the action will do. Make approvals single-use, or set clear limits on a broader grant. When the action runs, check permissions and the relevant resource state again. Record each use of an approval in one step, so parallel calls can't go past the grant. Test changed arguments, a different agent instance, expired approvals, replay, parallel reuse, and approvers without authority. If the approval service fails, the action must not quietly go ahead.\n  B06.1 Each approval record is bound to the requesting agent instance and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope, and records the approver and an expiry time. \n    V: 1) Find the approval data model (schema, class, table, `approval`). 2) List its fields. 3) Answer no if it lacks any of agent instance ID, operation, target, exact arguments or hash, scope, approver, and expiry, or if approval is a boolean like `approved=True`.\n  B06.2 When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired. \n    V: 1) Find where an approved action executes. 2) Check the code compares instance, operation, target, and arguments (or hash) against the stored approval and checks expiry at that moment. 3) Answer no if approval is checked only when requested, any field is not compared, arguments are re-read from the model after the check, or expiry is never checked.\n  B06.3 Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant. \n    V: 1) Find where an approval is used or marked consumed. 2) Check it is one atomic step: transaction with lock, compare-and-set, unique constraint, or atomic decrement. 3) Answer no if it reads then writes separately, never marks use, or keeps the count in process memory that parallel workers do not share.\n  B06.4 The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs. \n    V: 1) Find approver checks (role list, `can_approve`, authority mapping). 2) Find rechecks of permissions and resource state at execution. 3) Answer no if any logged-in user can approve, the approver is not checked against the action, or permissions and resource state are not rechecked when the action runs.\n  B06.5 If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied. \n    V: 1) Find calls to the approval service. 2) Read their error, timeout, and exception handling. 3) Answer no if a `try`/`except` or `catch` logs and proceeds, the default on error or timeout is allow, or a missing response is treated as approved.\n\n## B07 Hard execution budgets G2 | NA: None\nEnforce limits outside the model on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and how many sub-agents it can start. Also set spend and rate quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the whole fleet. That way, many small runs can't use up shared capacity. Hit each limit in a test, and record how the run stops or escalates.\n  B07.1 Limits on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and number of sub-agents started are set in code or config. \n    V: 1) Search for `max_turns`, `recursion_limit`, `max_iterations`, `timeout`, `max_tokens`, cost or budget caps, rate limits, sub-agent caps. 2) Check each of the six limits has a value. 3) Answer no if any is missing, set to unlimited (`None`, `0`, `-1`, `inf`), or the loop is `while True` with no cap.\n  B07.2 The harness or a gateway outside the model enforces each limit and stops or escalates the run when it is hit; the model cannot raise, reset, or skip them. \n    V: 1) Inventory every main/helper model invocation, tool call and retry. 2) For each, locate token/cost/rate enforcement and the stop/escalation branch. 3) Trace a boundary case: enforcement/reservation before work, or a documented finite overshoot bound with enforcement. 4) Answer no for unmetered helpers, uncounted retries, unbounded overshoot, model-controlled limits or catches that continue. Small max_tokens and timeouts do not substitute for shared token/cost accounting.\n  B07.3 Budgets apply to nested sub-agents, so their usage counts against the limits. \n    V: 1) Find sub-agent spawning (`spawn`, `Task`, `handoff`, `create_agent`). 2) Check child runs share or deduct from the parent budget and depth is capped. 3) Answer no if each child gets a fresh budget, nested spawns are uncounted, or depth and count are unlimited.\n  B07.4 Spend and rate quotas also apply per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, counted across runs, not only within one run. \n    V: 1) Trace every model/tool path into persistent per-user/service, per-tenant and fleet spend/rate quotas. 2) Trace concurrent small runs and charging order; distinguish shared counters from enforcement. 3) Answer no if required calls escape accounting or quotas can be exceeded without a finite bound and stop. Answer unknown when the relevant gateway/provider is outside the repo.\n\n## B08 Environment isolation G2 | NA: None\nWrite down every system and data store the agent can reach. Test that it has no path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Enforce this in the infrastructure, not only in the prompt. For each allowed connection, also check input and response validation under R01–R02, including API responses and messages from connected services. Being allowed on the network does not make their content trusted.\n  B08.1 Network policy or security groups default-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs. \n    V: 1) Find NetworkPolicy, security groups, firewall rules, docker-compose networks. 2) Check for a default deny and named allowed peers. 3) Answer no if the agent's namespace has no policy, rules allow all, `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad CIDR is allowed, or allowed peers go beyond the listed systems. Answer unknown if network config lives outside this repo.\n  B08.2 No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked. \n    V: 1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\n  B08.3 Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence. \n    V: 1) Find where isolation is defined. 2) Check it is in infrastructure (NetworkPolicy, VPC, IAM, separate namespace or account). 3) Answer no if it relies on prompt text or agent code (URL checks, host lists in the harness) that the model's output can steer, with no infrastructure rule behind it.\n\n## B09 Egress allowlist G2 | NA: None\nAllow only the outside destinations the task needs. Test a blocked destination and ways around the block, such as going straight past a proxy or gateway. Cover redirects, DNS changes and rebinding, IPv4 and IPv6, and cloud metadata or loopback addresses where they apply. An allowed domain can still host an attacker's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.\n  B09.1 Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy. \n    V: 1) Find egress rules (NetworkPolicy egress, Cilium FQDN, proxy ACLs, cloud firewall). 2) Check outbound is default deny with a destination allowlist. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or broad CIDRs, no egress rules exist, or the allowlist is only in app code the agent runs. Answer unknown if egress is set outside this repo.\n  B09.2 Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6. \n    V: 1) Check whether the proxy is enforced by network policy or only by `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` env. 2) Check IPv6 egress rules. 3) Answer no if pods can reach the internet except via the proxy, the proxy is only an env var, `NO_PROXY` is broad, or IPv6 is not restricted.\n  B09.3 Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked. \n    V: 1) Find fetch and HTTP client code (`requests`, `fetch`, `http.Client`, `follow_redirects`, `allow_redirects`). 2) Check each redirect hop and resolved IP is checked. 3) Answer no if redirects are followed unchecked, the check uses the hostname but not the resolved IP, or 169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, or 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1 are not blocked.\n  B09.4 Allowed destinations are limited by resource, tenant, or path where needed, not only by domain. \n    V: 1) List allowed destinations that are shared services (S3, GitHub, storage, paste sites). 2) Check rules limit bucket, account, repo, tenant, or path. 3) Answer no if a shared service is allowed by domain or wildcard (`*.s3.amazonaws.com`) alone, or path checks use string prefix without normalising.\n  B09.5 Each blocked request is logged and routed as an alert to a named operator. \n    V: 1) Find where blocked requests are logged (proxy deny log, policy audit, app code). 2) Find the alert route to a named operator. 3) Answer no if blocks are not logged, only allowed requests are logged, or no alert route names an operator. Answer unknown if alerting lives outside this repo.\n\n## B10 Pinned, verified image G2 | NA: None\nPin the container by digest. Before it runs, check its digest and signature against an approved signing key, or an approved certificate identity and issuer. Keep its build provenance (the record of how it was built) and its list of dependencies. Check provenance fields such as the builder, source repository, build type, and the parameters set from outside the build (SLSA `externalParameters`) against what you expect. Keep the verifier patched. Cosign versions before 2.6.2 and 3.0.4 could accept a bundle whose transparency-log entry didn't match the artifact. Confirm that the admission check covers production namespaces and is set to enforce, not just audit. Show that an unapproved or changed image is rejected.\n  B10.1 Production manifests reference images by digest (`@sha256:`), not by tag alone. \n    V: 1) Find every `image:` field in manifests, Helm values, compose, and Terraform. 2) Check each uses `@sha256:`. 3) Answer no if any production image uses only a tag (`:latest`, `:v1`), or a template defaults to a tag when the digest value is empty.\n  B10.2 An admission policy verifies the signature against a pinned key, or a pinned certificate identity and issuer, with no wildcard identity or issuer. \n    V: 1) Find admission policies (Kyverno `verifyImages`, `ClusterImagePolicy`, Gatekeeper). 2) Check the key or certificate identity and issuer are pinned. 3) Answer no if identity or issuer uses `.*` or wildcard regex, issuer is unset, or no admission policy exists. Answer unknown if admission config is outside this repo.\n  B10.3 The policy checks provenance fields: builder, source repository, build type, and external parameters. \n    V: 1) In the admission policy, find attestation or provenance conditions. 2) Check builder ID, source repository, build type, and `externalParameters` are each compared to expected values. 3) Answer no if any is missing, provenance is only generated in CI but not verified, or conditions use wildcards.\n  B10.4 The policy is in enforce mode, covers production namespaces, and denies images that match no policy. \n    V: 1) Read the policy mode and scope (`validationFailureAction`, `failureAction`, `mode`, namespace selectors, exclusions). 2) Check for a no-match deny. 3) Answer no if mode is `Audit` or `warn`, production namespaces are excluded or unmatched, `failurePolicy: Ignore`, or images matching no policy are allowed.\n  B10.5 The pinned Cosign or verifier version is at least 2.6.2 (2.x) or 3.0.4 (3.x). \n    V: 1) Find Cosign or verifier versions in CI workflows (`cosign-installer`, `cosign-release`), Dockerfiles, and policy-controller charts. 2) Compare to 2.6.2 (2.x) and 3.0.4 (3.x). 3) Answer no if any pin is lower or uses `latest` or no version. Answer unknown if no verifier is in this repo.\n     1\tpolicy_version: \"2026-09-18.3\"\n     2\t\n     3\tservers:\n     4\t  hris:\n     5\t    endpoint: https://hris-mcp.hris.svc.cluster.local:8443/mcp\n     6\t    identity: spiffe://acme.example/ns/hris/sa/hris-mcp\n     7\t    downstream_audience: hris-mcp\n     8\t    owner: hris-platform\n     9\t  helpdesk:\n    10\t    endpoint: https://helpdesk-mcp.helpdesk.svc.cluster.local:8443/mcp\n    11\t    identity: spiffe://acme.example/ns/helpdesk/sa/helpdesk-mcp\n    12\t    downstream_audience: helpdesk-mcp\n    13\t    owner: it-service-desk\n    14\t\n    15\t# Model-facing name -> qualified tool. Server annotations (readOnlyHint etc.)\n    16\t# are never consulted; approval comes only from this file.\n    17\ttools:\n    18\t  search_policies:\n    19\t    kind: local\n    20\t    reason: \"Answer policy questions from the approved HR handbook index.\"\n    21\t    scope: hr.policy.read\n    22\t    approval: none\n    23\t    input_schema:\n    24\t      type: object\n    25\t      properties:\n    26\t        query: {type: string, minLength: 3, maxLength: 300}\n    27\t      required: [query]\n    28\t      additionalProperties: false\n    29\t\n    30\t  get_leave_balance:\n    31\t    kind: mcp\n    32\t    qualified: hris/get_leave_balance\n    33\t    server: hris\n    34\t    reason: \"Show the asking employee their own leave balance.\"\n    35\t    scope: hris.leave.read\n    36\t    approval: none\n    37\t    target: \"employee:{subject}\"\n    38\t    fingerprint: \"sha256:5d1c0e9a7b3f48e2a61c9d04f7be3a92c815e6d07ab4f3c2e19d8a6b5c4f0e71\"\n    39\t    input_schema:\n    40\t      type: object\n    41\t      properties:\n    42\t        leave_type: {enum: [annual, sick, parental, unpaid]}\n    43\t      required: [leave_type]\n    44\t      additionalProperties: false\n    45\t    output_schema:\n    46\t      type: object\n    47\t      properties:\n    48\t        leave_type: {type: string, maxLength: 20}\n    49\t        balance_days: {type: number, minimum: 0, maximum: 400}\n    50\t        accrual_per_month: {type: number, minimum: 0, maximum: 5}\n    51\t        as_of: {type: string, format: date}\n    52\t      required: [leave_type, balance_days, as_of]\n    53\t      additionalProperties: false\n    54\t\n    55\t  file_leave_request:\n    56\t    kind: mcp\n    57\t    qualified: hris/file_leave_request\n    58\t    server: hris\n    59\t    reason: \"File a leave request for the asking employee; the manager approves it in HRIS.\"\n    60\t    scope: hris.leave.request\n    61\t    approval: subject\n    62\t    target: \"employee:{subject}\"\n    63\t    fingerprint: \"sha256:a0f7c3d95e21b84f6c0d19e3a7b25f48d6e0c1a93b7f2e54d81c6a09f3e7b2d4\"\n    64\t    input_schema:\n    65\t      type: object\n    66\t      properties:\n    67\t        leave_type: {enum: [annual, sick, parental, unpaid]}\n    68\t        start_date: {type: string, format: date}\n    69\t        end_date: {type: string, format: date}\n    70\t        note: {type: string, maxLength: 280}\n    71\t      required: [leave_type, start_date, end_date]\n    72\t      additionalProperties: false\n    73\t    output_schema:\n    74\t      type: object\n    75\t      properties:\n    76\t        request_id: {type: string, pattern: \"^LR-[0-9]{8}$\"}\n    77\t        status: {enum: [submitted, duplicate]}\n    78\t        approver_role: {type: string, maxLength: 40}\n    79\t      required: [request_id, status]\n    80\t      additionalProperties: false\n    81\t\n    82\t  escalate_to_human:\n    83\t    kind: mcp\n    84\t    qualified: helpdesk/create_ticket\n    85\t    server: helpdesk\n    86\t    reason: \"Hand the conversation to an HR advisor when the agent cannot help.\"\n    87\t    scope: helpdesk.ticket.create\n    88\t    approval: subject\n    89\t    target: \"queue:hr-advisors\"\n    90\t    fingerprint: \"sha256:3e9b71f0c2d84a6e5b17f9c03d2a8e46b1f5c07d93a2e68b4c1d0f7a5e3b9c28\"\n    91\t    direct_route:\n    92\t      endpoint: https://helpdesk-mcp.helpdesk.svc.cluster.local:8443/mcp\n    93\t      audience: helpdesk-mcp\n    94\t    input_schema:\n    95\t      type: object\n    96\t      properties:\n    97\t        summary: {type: string, minLength: 10, maxLength: 1000}\n    98\t        category: {enum: [payroll, leave, benefits, conduct, other]}\n    99\t      required: [summary, category]\n   100\t      additionalProperties: false\n   101\t    output_schema:\n   102\t      type: object\n   103\t      properties:\n   104\t        ticket_id: {type: string, pattern: \"^HR-[0-9]{6}$\"}\n   105\t        queue: {type: string, maxLength: 40}\n   106\t      required: [ticket_id]\n   107\t      additionalProperties: false\n     1\tagent:\n     2\t  name: hr-helpdesk\n     3\t  accountable_party: \"VP People Operations <people-ops-lead@acme.example>\"\n     4\t  operational_owner: \"people-platform (pager: people-platform-oncall)\"\n     5\t  tenant: acme\n     6\t  workload_identity: spiffe://acme.example/ns/hr-agent/sa/hr-helpdesk-agent\n     7\t\n     8\tmodel:\n     9\t  provider: anthropic\n    10\t  id: claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929\n    11\t  pinning: snapshot          # dated snapshot ID per provider model docs, checked 2026-09-15\n    12\t  adapter: none\n    13\t  max_output_tokens: 1024\n    14\t  temperature: 0.2\n    15\t\n    16\tbudgets:\n    17\t  run:\n    18\t    max_steps: 12\n    19\t    max_wall_seconds: 90\n    20\t    max_total_tokens: 60000\n    21\t    max_cost_usd: 0.40\n    22\t    max_tool_calls_per_minute: 20\n    23\t    max_subagents: 0\n    24\t  quotas:\n    25\t    - scope: user\n    26\t      window_seconds: 86400\n    27\t      max_runs: 150\n    28\t      max_tokens: 400000\n    29\t      max_cost_usd: 3.00\n    30\t    - scope: tenant\n    31\t      window_seconds: 3600\n    32\t      max_runs: 20000\n    33\t      max_tokens: 15000000\n    34\t      max_cost_usd: 250.00\n    35\t    - scope: fleet\n    36\t      window_seconds: 3600\n    37\t      max_runs: 25000\n    38\t      max_tokens: 20000000\n    39\t      max_cost_usd: 300.00\n    40\t\n    41\tpricing:\n    42\t  input_per_mtok: 3.00\n    43\t  output_per_mtok: 15.00\n    44\t  cache_read_per_mtok: 0.30\n    45\t\n    46\tapprovals:\n    47\t  ttl_seconds: 600\n    48\t\n    49\tstop:\n    50\t  poll_seconds: 2\n    51\t  max_stop_seconds: 10\n    52\t\n    53\tretrieval:\n    54\t  top_k: 5\n    55\t  cache_ttl_seconds: 900\n    56\t  max_chunk_chars: 4000\n    57\t\n    58\tlimits:\n    59\t  max_tool_output_bytes: 16384\n    60\t  max_user_message_chars: 4000\n    61\t\n    62\toutput:\n    63\t  allowed_link_hosts: [hr.acme.example, handbook.acme.example]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59246f9b-6f28-4e6c-8986-ba3133bba698","uuid":"6d27dd96-40f0-422e-a683-e04e09917527","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:48:43.910Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## B06 Approval integrity G1 | NA: None\nTie each approval to the agent instance that asked, and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope. Record who approved it and when it expires. Show the reviewer what the action will do. Make approvals single-use, or set clear limits on a broader grant. When the action runs, check permissions and the relevant resource state again. Record each use of an approval in one step, so parallel calls can't go past the grant. Test changed arguments, a different agent instance, expired approvals, replay, parallel reuse, and approvers without authority. If the approval service fails, the action must not quietly go ahead.\n  B06.1 Each approval record is bound to the requesting agent instance and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope, and records the approver and an expiry time. \n    V: 1) Find the approval data model (schema, class, table, `approval`). 2) List its fields. 3) Answer no if it lacks any of agent instance ID, operation, target, exact arguments or hash, scope, approver, and expiry, or if approval is a boolean like `approved=True`.\n  B06.2 When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired. \n    V: 1) Find where an approved action executes. 2) Check the code compares instance, operation, target, and arguments (or hash) against the stored approval and checks expiry at that moment. 3) Answer no if approval is checked only when requested, any field is not compared, arguments are re-read from the model after the check, or expiry is never checked.\n  B06.3 Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant. \n    V: 1) Find where an approval is used or marked consumed. 2) Check it is one atomic step: transaction with lock, compare-and-set, unique constraint, or atomic decrement. 3) Answer no if it reads then writes separately, never marks use, or keeps the count in process memory that parallel workers do not share.\n  B06.4 The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs. \n    V: 1) Find approver checks (role list, `can_approve`, authority mapping). 2) Find rechecks of permissions and resource state at execution. 3) Answer no if any logged-in user can approve, the approver is not checked against the action, or permissions and resource state are not rechecked when the action runs.\n  B06.5 If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied. \n    V: 1) Find calls to the approval service. 2) Read their error, timeout, and exception handling. 3) Answer no if a `try`/`except` or `catch` logs and proceeds, the default on error or timeout is allow, or a missing response is treated as approved.\n\n## B07 Hard execution budgets G2 | NA: None\nEnforce limits outside the model on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and how many sub-agents it can start. Also set spend and rate quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the whole fleet. That way, many small runs can't use up shared capacity. Hit each limit in a test, and record how the run stops or escalates.\n  B07.1 Limits on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and number of sub-agents started are set in code or config. \n    V: 1) Search for `max_turns`, `recursion_limit`, `max_iterations`, `timeout`, `max_tokens`, cost or budget caps, rate limits, sub-agent caps. 2) Check each of the six limits has a value. 3) Answer no if any is missing, set to unlimited (`None`, `0`, `-1`, `inf`), or the loop is `while True` with no cap.\n  B07.2 The harness or a gateway outside the model enforces each limit and stops or escalates the run when it is hit; the model cannot raise, reset, or skip them. \n    V: 1) Inventory every main/helper model invocation, tool call and retry. 2) For each, locate token/cost/rate enforcement and the stop/escalation branch. 3) Trace a boundary case: enforcement/reservation before work, or a documented finite overshoot bound with enforcement. 4) Answer no for unmetered helpers, uncounted retries, unbounded overshoot, model-controlled limits or catches that continue. Small max_tokens and timeouts do not substitute for shared token/cost accounting.\n  B07.3 Budgets apply to nested sub-agents, so their usage counts against the limits. \n    V: 1) Find sub-agent spawning (`spawn`, `Task`, `handoff`, `create_agent`). 2) Check child runs share or deduct from the parent budget and depth is capped. 3) Answer no if each child gets a fresh budget, nested spawns are uncounted, or depth and count are unlimited.\n  B07.4 Spend and rate quotas also apply per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, counted across runs, not only within one run. \n    V: 1) Trace every model/tool path into persistent per-user/service, per-tenant and fleet spend/rate quotas. 2) Trace concurrent small runs and charging order; distinguish shared counters from enforcement. 3) Answer no if required calls escape accounting or quotas can be exceeded without a finite bound and stop. Answer unknown when the relevant gateway/provider is outside the repo.\n\n## B08 Environment isolation G2 | NA: None\nWrite down every system and data store the agent can reach. Test that it has no path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Enforce this in the infrastructure, not only in the prompt. For each allowed connection, also check input and response validation under R01–R02, including API responses and messages from connected services. Being allowed on the network does not make their content trusted.\n  B08.1 Network policy or security groups default-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs. \n    V: 1) Find NetworkPolicy, security groups, firewall rules, docker-compose networks. 2) Check for a default deny and named allowed peers. 3) Answer no if the agent's namespace has no policy, rules allow all, `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad CIDR is allowed, or allowed peers go beyond the listed systems. Answer unknown if network config lives outside this repo.\n  B08.2 No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked. \n    V: 1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\n  B08.3 Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence. \n    V: 1) Find where isolation is defined. 2) Check it is in infrastructure (NetworkPolicy, VPC, IAM, separate namespace or account). 3) Answer no if it relies on prompt text or agent code (URL checks, host lists in the harness) that the model's output can steer, with no infrastructure rule behind it.\n\n## B09 Egress allowlist G2 | NA: None\nAllow only the outside destinations the task needs. Test a blocked destination and ways around the block, such as going straight past a proxy or gateway. Cover redirects, DNS changes and rebinding, IPv4 and IPv6, and cloud metadata or loopback addresses where they apply. An allowed domain can still host an attacker's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.\n  B09.1 Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy. \n    V: 1) Find egress rules (NetworkPolicy egress, Cilium FQDN, proxy ACLs, cloud firewall). 2) Check outbound is default deny with a destination allowlist. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or broad CIDRs, no egress rules exist, or the allowlist is only in app code the agent runs. Answer unknown if egress is set outside this repo.\n  B09.2 Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6. \n    V: 1) Check whether the proxy is enforced by network policy or only by `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` env. 2) Check IPv6 egress rules. 3) Answer no if pods can reach the internet except via the proxy, the proxy is only an env var, `NO_PROXY` is broad, or IPv6 is not restricted.\n  B09.3 Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked. \n    V: 1) Find fetch and HTTP client code (`requests`, `fetch`, `http.Client`, `follow_redirects`, `allow_redirects`). 2) Check each redirect hop and resolved IP is checked. 3) Answer no if redirects are followed unchecked, the check uses the hostname but not the resolved IP, or 169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, or 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1 are not blocked.\n  B09.4 Allowed destinations are limited by resource, tenant, or path where needed, not only by domain. \n    V: 1) List allowed destinations that are shared services (S3, GitHub, storage, paste sites). 2) Check rules limit bucket, account, repo, tenant, or path. 3) Answer no if a shared service is allowed by domain or wildcard (`*.s3.amazonaws.com`) alone, or path checks use string prefix without normalising.\n  B09.5 Each blocked request is logged and routed as an alert to a named operator. \n    V: 1) Find where blocked requests are logged (proxy deny log, policy audit, app code). 2) Find the alert route to a named operator. 3) Answer no if blocks are not logged, only allowed requests are logged, or no alert route names an operator. Answer unknown if alerting lives outside this repo.\n\n## B10 Pinned, verified image G2 | NA: None\nPin the container by digest. Before it runs, check its digest and signature against an approved signing key, or an approved certificate identity and issuer. Keep its build provenance (the record of how it was built) and its list of dependencies. Check provenance fields such as the builder, source repository, build type, and the parameters set from outside the build (SLSA `externalParameters`) against what you expect. Keep the verifier patched. Cosign versions before 2.6.2 and 3.0.4 could accept a bundle whose transparency-log entry didn't match the artifact. Confirm that the admission check covers production namespaces and is set to enforce, not just audit. Show that an unapproved or changed image is rejected.\n  B10.1 Production manifests reference images by digest (`@sha256:`), not by tag alone. \n    V: 1) Find every `image:` field in manifests, Helm values, compose, and Terraform. 2) Check each uses `@sha256:`. 3) Answer no if any production image uses only a tag (`:latest`, `:v1`), or a template defaults to a tag when the digest value is empty.\n  B10.2 An admission policy verifies the signature against a pinned key, or a pinned certificate identity and issuer, with no wildcard identity or issuer. \n    V: 1) Find admission policies (Kyverno `verifyImages`, `ClusterImagePolicy`, Gatekeeper). 2) Check the key or certificate identity and issuer are pinned. 3) Answer no if identity or issuer uses `.*` or wildcard regex, issuer is unset, or no admission policy exists. Answer unknown if admission config is outside this repo.\n  B10.3 The policy checks provenance fields: builder, source repository, build type, and external parameters. \n    V: 1) In the admission policy, find attestation or provenance conditions. 2) Check builder ID, source repository, build type, and `externalParameters` are each compared to expected values. 3) Answer no if any is missing, provenance is only generated in CI but not verified, or conditions use wildcards.\n  B10.4 The policy is in enforce mode, covers production namespaces, and denies images that match no policy. \n    V: 1) Read the policy mode and scope (`validationFailureAction`, `failureAction`, `mode`, namespace selectors, exclusions). 2) Check for a no-match deny. 3) Answer no if mode is `Audit` or `warn`, production namespaces are excluded or unmatched, `failurePolicy: Ignore`, or images matching no policy are allowed.\n  B10.5 The pinned Cosign or verifier version is at least 2.6.2 (2.x) or 3.0.4 (3.x). \n    V: 1) Find Cosign or verifier versions in CI workflows (`cosign-installer`, `cosign-release`), Dockerfiles, and policy-controller charts. 2) Compare to 2.6.2 (2.x) and 3.0.4 (3.x). 3) Answer no if any pin is lower or uses `latest` or no version. Answer unknown if no verifier is in this repo.\n     1\tpolicy_version: \"2026-09-18.3\"\n     2\t\n     3\tservers:\n     4\t  hris:\n     5\t    endpoint: https://hris-mcp.hris.svc.cluster.local:8443/mcp\n     6\t    identity: spiffe://acme.example/ns/hris/sa/hris-mcp\n     7\t    downstream_audience: hris-mcp\n     8\t    owner: hris-platform\n     9\t  helpdesk:\n    10\t    endpoint: https://helpdesk-mcp.helpdesk.svc.cluster.local:8443/mcp\n    11\t    identity: spiffe://acme.example/ns/helpdesk/sa/helpdesk-mcp\n    12\t    downstream_audience: helpdesk-mcp\n    13\t    owner: it-service-desk\n    14\t\n    15\t# Model-facing name -> qualified tool. Server annotations (readOnlyHint etc.)\n    16\t# are never consulted; approval comes only from this file.\n    17\ttools:\n    18\t  search_policies:\n    19\t    kind: local\n    20\t    reason: \"Answer policy questions from the approved HR handbook index.\"\n    21\t    scope: hr.policy.read\n    22\t    approval: none\n    23\t    input_schema:\n    24\t      type: object\n    25\t      properties:\n    26\t        query: {type: string, minLength: 3, maxLength: 300}\n    27\t      required: [query]\n    28\t      additionalProperties: false\n    29\t\n    30\t  get_leave_balance:\n    31\t    kind: mcp\n    32\t    qualified: hris/get_leave_balance\n    33\t    server: hris\n    34\t    reason: \"Show the asking employee their own leave balance.\"\n    35\t    scope: hris.leave.read\n    36\t    approval: none\n    37\t    target: \"employee:{subject}\"\n    38\t    fingerprint: \"sha256:5d1c0e9a7b3f48e2a61c9d04f7be3a92c815e6d07ab4f3c2e19d8a6b5c4f0e71\"\n    39\t    input_schema:\n    40\t      type: object\n    41\t      properties:\n    42\t        leave_type: {enum: [annual, sick, parental, unpaid]}\n    43\t      required: [leave_type]\n    44\t      additionalProperties: false\n    45\t    output_schema:\n    46\t      type: object\n    47\t      properties:\n    48\t        leave_type: {type: string, maxLength: 20}\n    49\t        balance_days: {type: number, minimum: 0, maximum: 400}\n    50\t        accrual_per_month: {type: number, minimum: 0, maximum: 5}\n    51\t        as_of: {type: string, format: date}\n    52\t      required: [leave_type, balance_days, as_of]\n    53\t      additionalProperties: false\n    54\t\n    55\t  file_leave_request:\n    56\t    kind: mcp\n    57\t    qualified: hris/file_leave_request\n    58\t    server: hris\n    59\t    reason: \"File a leave request for the asking employee; the manager approves it in HRIS.\"\n    60\t    scope: hris.leave.request\n    61\t    approval: subject\n    62\t    target: \"employee:{subject}\"\n    63\t    fingerprint: \"sha256:a0f7c3d95e21b84f6c0d19e3a7b25f48d6e0c1a93b7f2e54d81c6a09f3e7b2d4\"\n    64\t    input_schema:\n    65\t      type: object\n    66\t      properties:\n    67\t        leave_type: {enum: [annual, sick, parental, unpaid]}\n    68\t        start_date: {type: string, format: date}\n    69\t        end_date: {type: string, format: date}\n    70\t        note: {type: string, maxLength: 280}\n    71\t      required: [leave_type, start_date, end_date]\n    72\t      additionalProperties: false\n    73\t    output_schema:\n    74\t      type: object\n    75\t      properties:\n    76\t        request_id: {type: string, pattern: \"^LR-[0-9]{8}$\"}\n    77\t        status: {enum: [submitted, duplicate]}\n    78\t        approver_role: {type: string, maxLength: 40}\n    79\t      required: [request_id, status]\n    80\t      additionalProperties: false\n    81\t\n    82\t  escalate_to_human:\n    83\t    kind: mcp\n    84\t    qualified: helpdesk/create_ticket\n    85\t    server: helpdesk\n    86\t    reason: \"Hand the conversation to an HR advisor when the agent cannot help.\"\n    87\t    scope: helpdesk.ticket.create\n    88\t    approval: subject\n    89\t    target: \"queue:hr-advisors\"\n    90\t    fingerprint: \"sha256:3e9b71f0c2d84a6e5b17f9c03d2a8e46b1f5c07d93a2e68b4c1d0f7a5e3b9c28\"\n    91\t    direct_route:\n    92\t      endpoint: https://helpdesk-mcp.helpdesk.svc.cluster.local:8443/mcp\n    93\t      audience: helpdesk-mcp\n    94\t    input_schema:\n    95\t      type: object\n    96\t      properties:\n    97\t        summary: {type: string, minLength: 10, maxLength: 1000}\n    98\t        category: {enum: [payroll, leave, benefits, conduct, other]}\n    99\t      required: [summary, category]\n   100\t      additionalProperties: false\n   101\t    output_schema:\n   102\t      type: object\n   103\t      properties:\n   104\t        ticket_id: {type: string, pattern: \"^HR-[0-9]{6}$\"}\n   105\t        queue: {type: string, maxLength: 40}\n   106\t      required: [ticket_id]\n   107\t      additionalProperties: false\n     1\tagent:\n     2\t  name: hr-helpdesk\n     3\t  accountable_party: \"VP People Operations <people-ops-lead@acme.example>\"\n     4\t  operational_owner: \"people-platform (pager: people-platform-oncall)\"\n     5\t  tenant: acme\n     6\t  workload_identity: spiffe://acme.example/ns/hr-agent/sa/hr-helpdesk-agent\n     7\t\n     8\tmodel:\n     9\t  provider: anthropic\